## Why This Is Asked
Tests advanced 128-bit bit-twiddling, boundary shifts, and correctness under rotation.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values represented as hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Rotation across a 64-bit boundary
- Handling n in [0,127] with minimal branching in production

## Code Example
```javascript
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
  n &= 127; // modulo 128
  if (n < 64){
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  } else {
    unsigned k = n - 64;
    uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
    uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can you make it fully branchless with masks?
- How would you adapt for different word sizes or endianness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply a classic bit-twiddling pattern (popcount) to a 128-bit type by handling two 64-bit halves, staying branchless and efficient.

## Key Concepts
- Hamming weight via masks and shifts
- Handling composite 128-bit types (hi/lo halves)
- Branchless aggregation of results

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef unsigned long long ull;
typedef struct { ull hi; ull lo; } uint128;

static inline unsigned long long popcount64(ull x){
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return x & 0x7F;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x){
  return (unsigned int)(popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests advanced bit manipulation skills, requiring multi-word arithmetic without 128-bit native support. It demonstrates branchless programming techniques and careful handling of shift operations.

## Key Concepts
- Represent 128-bit values as two 64-bit words (hi, lo)
- Decompose k into t = k mod 64 (intra-word shift) and b = k div 64 (cross-word flag)
- Branchless selection using bitwise operations and masks
- Handle edge cases like k=0 and k=64 without conditionals

## Code Example
```c
void rotate_left_128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned k, 
                    uint64_t* out_hi, uint64_t* out_lo) {
    uint64_t t = k & 63;
    uint64_t b = (k >> 6) & 1;

    uint64_t hiA = (hi << t) | (lo >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t loA = (lo << t) | (hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t hiB = (lo << t) | (hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t loB = (hi << t) | (lo >> (64 - t));

    *out_hi = hiA ^ ((hiA ^ hiB) & -b);
    *out_lo = loA ^ ((loA ^ loB) & -b);
}
```

## Examples
- k=1, hi=0, lo=1 → out_hi=1, out_lo=0
- k=64, hi=1, lo=0 → out_hi=0, out_lo=1

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft branchless bit-twiddling in 128-bit space, a common low-level optimization task.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit integer handling via two 64-bit halves
- Trailing-zero count (ctz) tricks without branches
- De Bruijn sequences for fast bit indexing

## Code Example
```c
unsigned countTrailingZeros128(uint128 x) {
  static const unsigned char deBruijn64[64] = {
    0, 1, 2, 53, 3, 7, 54, 27,
    4, 38, 8, 34, 55, 48, 28, 20,
    5, 39, 53, 6, 59, 29, 42, 16,
    9, 35, 60, 50, 45, 27, 21, 17,
    10, 49, 56, 40, 30, 14, 43, 15,
    11, 23, 51, 52, 41, 19, 13, 44,
    12, 26, 58, 36, 31, 37, 29, 22
  };
  unsigned loIdx = ((x.lo & -x.lo) * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ull) >> 58;
  unsigned hiIdx = ((x.hi & -x.hi) * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ull) >> 58;
  unsigned tzLo = deBruijn64[loIdx];
  unsigned tzHi = deBruijn64[hiIdx];
  unsigned isLoZero = (x.lo == 0);
  return (1 - isLoZero) * tzLo + isLoZero * (64 + tzHi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support a variable-width 128-bit type with non-power-of-two lanes?
- Compare performance of this approach against a masked binary search approach on modern CPUs.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason with 128-bit data layout and perform blockwise rearrangements using only bitwise ops. It also checks handling of multi-word operands without branching.

## Key Concepts

- Block masking and extraction
- Cross-word recombination
- Branchless 128-bit manipulation

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 swapAdjacentBlocks128(uint128 x, unsigned block) {
    uint64_t M = (1ULL << block) - 1;
    uint64_t A0 = x.lo & M;
    uint64_t A1 = (x.lo >> block) & M;
    uint64_t A2 = x.hi & M;
    uint64_t A3 = (x.hi >> block) & M;
    uint64_t loP = (A0 << block) | A1;
    uint64_t hiP = (A2 << block) | A3;
    return (uint128){.hi = hiP, .lo = loP};
}
```

